---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PrestigeImage from '../components/PrestigeImage.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Human Algorithm — Origin Story | Scott Bales"
  description="There is an algorithm no one talks about in boardrooms. Jenson taught me who technology forgets. Isaac is what happens when that generation refuses to be forgotten. Esther is the outcome."
>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACT I: THE HUMAN ALGORITHM — Jenson
       Full viewport. Open on black. Let the reader meet him
       before they meet the idea.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center px-8">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center">
        <p class="font-sans text-xs tracking-[0.2em] uppercase text-accent mb-8 fade-in">Act I</p>
        <h1 class="font-serif text-5xl md:text-7xl lg:text-8xl leading-[1.05] mb-12 fade-in">
          The Human Algorithm
        </h1>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed fade-in">
          There is an algorithm no one talks about in boardrooms.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Jenson — The story begins -->
  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">

        <!-- Photo: Jenson — candid, not posed -->
        <PrestigeImage
          src="/images/jenson-candid.JPG"
          alt="Jenson — the person who changed everything"
          aspect="3/2"
          class="mb-12"
        />

        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          It does not optimize. It does not scale. It cannot be fine-tuned, benchmarked,
          or deployed. It is the set of assumptions — quiet, inherited, mostly unexamined —
          that determines who technology is built for, and who it leaves behind.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-8">
          I spent fifteen years in the technology industry before I understood this.
          My son taught me in an afternoon.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Jenson was born into a world that was not designed for him. The interfaces
          were wrong. The inputs assumed a user he would never be. The systems I had
          spent my career building — elegant, efficient, frictionless for millions —
          had nothing to say to my son.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed">
          He could not tap, swipe, type, or speak the way the products expected.
          And so the products ignored him.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-base text-gray-400 mt-8">
          This is not a metaphor. This is what happened.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pull-quote: Jenson -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-[60vh] flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center fade-in">
        <blockquote class="origin-pullquote font-serif text-3xl md:text-5xl leading-[1.15] text-accent">
          I was telling boardrooms to put humans first while the most important
          human in my life was showing me I didn't know what that meant.
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          I had built innovation strategies for banks on four continents. I had advised
          executives at Microsoft, Google, and Visa on how to meet their customers
          where they are. I had stood on stages and told audiences that technology
          must serve people.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Jenson sat in my living room and made all of it theoretical.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          The gap was not in my knowledge. It was in my premise. I had been optimizing
          for the average user. Jenson is not the average user. He is the edge case
          that reveals whether your system actually works — or whether it only works
          for people who were already fine without it.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mt-8">
          That distinction changes everything.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Statement: full viewport -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center fade-in">
        <p class="font-serif text-3xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl leading-[1.15] text-white">
          <strong>Technology's job is not efficiency. It is inclusion.</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="font-serif text-3xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl leading-[1.15] text-gray-400 mt-4">
          <strong>Efficiency is a feature. Inclusion is the function.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACT II: THE FRONT LINES
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center">
        <p class="font-sans text-xs tracking-[0.2em] uppercase text-accent mb-8 fade-in">Act II</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-5xl md:text-7xl leading-[1.05] fade-in">
          The Front Lines
        </h2>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">

        <!-- Photo: Stage shot or editorial portrait -->
        <PrestigeImage
          src="/images/scott-bales-stage-wide.jpg"
          alt="Scott Bales on stage — the front lines"
          aspect="16/9"
          class="mb-12"
        />

        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          I did not arrive at this understanding from the outside.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Before Jenson, there were twenty years on the front lines of every major
          shift in how people interact with money, information, and each other.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          At <strong class="text-white">WING Money</strong>, an ANZ subsidiary in Cambodia, I saw what happens when
          you build financial infrastructure for a population that has never had a
          bank account. The design constraints were absolute: low literacy, shared
          devices, intermittent connectivity. You could not default to the assumptions
          that worked in Sydney or Singapore. You had to start from the human and
          work backward to the technology.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          At <strong class="text-white">Visa</strong>, through Fundamo, I worked at the intersection of global payments
          infrastructure and emerging markets — where a mobile phone is not a convenience
          but the entire financial system. The question was never "how do we digitize
          what exists?" It was "what do we build for people the existing system
          never considered?"
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          At <strong class="text-white">Moven</strong>, I helped build the world's first downloadable bank. No branches.
          No paperwork. No legacy. A financial institution designed from a blank screen
          for a user who had never known anything else. We did not digitize banking.
          We asked what banking would look like if no one had invented it yet, and
          built that instead.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pull-quote: Front Lines -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-[60vh] flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center fade-in">
        <blockquote class="origin-pullquote font-serif text-3xl md:text-5xl leading-[1.15] text-accent">
          Three jobs. One question each time:<br />
          Who is this not working for — and what would it take to include them?
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Two books came out of those years. <em class="text-white">Mobile Ready</em> documented the behavioral
          shift of six billion people carrying connected devices — the untethered
          consumer who would not wait for institutions to catch up. <em class="text-white">Innovation Wars</em>
          gave corporate leaders the frameworks to stop defending old models and start
          building new ones.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          The thesis across both was the same: the organizations that survive are the
          ones that design for the person they have not met yet.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed">
          I did not know, when I wrote that, that the person I had not met yet
          was already in my house.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACT III: ISAAC
       Shift in rhythm. Shorter paragraphs. Present tense.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center">
        <p class="font-sans text-xs tracking-[0.2em] uppercase text-accent mb-8 fade-in">Act III</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-5xl md:text-7xl leading-[1.05] fade-in">
          <span class="text-accent">Isaac</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Isaac is nineteen.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          He has never walked into a bank. He has never filled out a form without
          asking why. He has never accepted a product that asked him to adapt to it
          rather than the other way around.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed">
          He is not hypothetical. He is the demographic reality sitting in your
          customer data, your employee pipeline, and your competitor's founding team.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Isaac grew up in Jenson's world.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Not literally — Isaac does not have Jenson's challenges. But he grew up in
          a culture that Jenson's generation forced into existence: one where
          accessibility is not a compliance checkbox but a baseline expectation. Where
          transparency is not a brand value but a filtering mechanism. Where inclusion
          is not a program — it is the minimum viable product.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed">
          Jenson required technology to include him. Isaac <em>expects</em> it.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-base text-gray-400 mt-8">
          That is the shift.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pull-quote: Isaac -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-[60vh] flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center fade-in">
        <blockquote class="origin-pullquote font-serif text-3xl md:text-5xl leading-[1.15] text-accent">
          Jenson taught me who technology forgets.<br />
          Isaac is what happens when that generation refuses to be forgotten.
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          The organizations I work with — the ones running AI strategies, redesigning
          customer journeys, rethinking workforce models — are preparing for a future
          defined by compute power, large language models, and automation. They are
          right to prepare.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-8">
          But they are preparing for the technology. They are not preparing for Isaac.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Isaac does not care about your AI strategy. He cares whether your AI strategy
          cares about him. He will not read your mission statement. He will test it —
          silently, instantly, and without giving you a second chance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Isaac's four criteria -->
  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-12">
          He evaluates every interaction against four criteria, whether he knows it or not:
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-8">
          <p class="font-serif text-2xl md:text-3xl text-white leading-snug">
            <strong class="text-accent">Transparency.</strong> Can I see how this decision was made?
          </p>
          <p class="font-serif text-2xl md:text-3xl text-white leading-snug">
            <strong class="text-accent">Autonomy.</strong> Can I control what happens to my data, my attention, my time?
          </p>
          <p class="font-serif text-2xl md:text-3xl text-white leading-snug">
            <strong class="text-accent">Immediacy.</strong> Does this meet me where I am, right now, on my terms?
          </p>
          <p class="font-serif text-2xl md:text-3xl text-white leading-snug">
            <strong class="text-accent">Inclusion.</strong> Was this built for someone like me — or was I an afterthought?
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mt-12">
          Fail one, and Isaac leaves. He does not complain. He does not churn visibly.
          He simply never arrives.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-8">
          That was the shift I warned about. Then the shift got a successor.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACT IV: ESTHER
       Colder. Clipped. Inevitable.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center">
        <p class="font-sans text-xs tracking-[0.2em] uppercase text-accent mb-8 fade-in">Act IV</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-5xl md:text-7xl leading-[1.05] fade-in">
          <span class="text-accent">Esther</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> was born on November&nbsp;30,&nbsp;2022.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed">
          So was ChatGPT.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          She will not remember a world before generative AI. She will not distinguish
          between "using a tool" and "having a conversation." She will not understand
          why your onboarding flow requires twelve steps when her AI agent completed
          it in one.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed">
          Isaac chose to leave. <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> will never arrive in the first place.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Isaac is the mobile native. The social native. The consumer who grew up
          with a screen in his hand and a swipe as his default verb. He is the shift
          that most organizations are still trying to absorb.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-8">
          <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> is what comes after absorption.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          She is the AI native. The agentic native. She does not interact with your
          product. Her agent does. She does not compare your service to a competitor.
          Her model does — instantly, across every alternative, with no loyalty
          and no memory of your brand promise.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pull-quote: Esther -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-[60vh] flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center fade-in">
        <blockquote class="origin-pullquote font-serif text-3xl md:text-5xl leading-[1.15] text-accent">
          Isaac disrupted your customer journey.<br />
          <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> dissolved it.
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The Generational Pincer -->
  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Here is the generational pincer that no strategy deck accounts for:
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Isaac is still in the room. He is your customer today, your mid-career
          employee, your most vocal critic on the platforms you still don't fully
          understand. He is not going away. He is getting more demanding.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-12">
          And behind him — three years old right now, learning language alongside
          a machine that learns it too — <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> is forming expectations that make
          Isaac's look quaint.
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-6 border-l border-accent/30 pl-8">
          <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
            Isaac expects inclusion. <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> expects intelligence.
          </p>
          <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
            Isaac expects transparency. <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> expects autonomy <em>delegated to an agent
            she trusts more than your interface.</em>
          </p>
          <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
            Isaac expects immediacy. <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> expects anticipation — the answer before
            the question, the solution before the friction.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-12">
          And inclusion? For <span class="text-accent">Esther</span>, inclusion is not a design principle.
          It is a fundamental right. Baked in. Non-negotiable. If Jenson cannot
          use it, it does not exist in <span class="text-accent">Esther</span>'s world. Not because she read a
          policy. Because her AI will refuse to route her to a product that
          excludes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Statement: Isaac / Esther — full viewport -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center fade-in">
        <p class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-6xl lg:text-7xl leading-[1.1] text-white">
          <strong><span class="text-accent">Isaac</span> defined the shift.</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-6xl lg:text-7xl leading-[1.1] text-accent mt-4">
          <strong><span class="text-accent">Esther</span> is the outcome.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          The Human Algorithm I described at the beginning of this story — the
          quiet set of assumptions that determines who technology serves — is being
          rewritten. Not by executives. Not by engineers. By a generation that will
          delegate the question entirely.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-8">
          <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> will not ask "was this built for me?"
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          Her agent will ask it for her. And if the answer is no, <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> will never
          know your company existed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLOSING: THE QUESTION
       Maximum whitespace. Single column. End of conversation.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          I have given keynotes on six continents. I have advised organizations
          through digital transformations that restructured how they operate, compete,
          and think. I have written books, built products, and sat in rooms where
          the decisions that shape industries get made.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-8">
          None of it would have happened the way it did without a boy who could not
          use the technology I was so proud of building.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          Jenson did not make me a better speaker. He made me a more honest one.
          He gave me a question I carried into every room for a decade. Isaac gave
          that question urgency. <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> gave it a deadline.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Final statement — full viewport -->
  <section class="origin-act min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center px-8 border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid w-full">
      <div class="content-col text-center fade-in">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-5xl md:text-7xl lg:text-8xl leading-[1.05]">
          <span class="text-accent">Isaac</span> defined your today.
        </h2>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-5xl md:text-7xl lg:text-8xl leading-[1.05] text-gray-400 mt-4">
          <span class="text-accent">Esther</span> will define your survival.
        </h2>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="origin-act section-pad border-t border-white/5">
    <div class="swiss-grid">
      <div class="content-col text-center fade-in">
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Your board has a strategy for AI.
        </p>
        <p class="font-sans text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Your team has a roadmap for transformation.
        </p>
